Perfect! Now I can see the issue clearly. Looking at lines 236-246 in segment-markdown.ts:

```typescript
if (FENCE.test(line)) {
  flush();
  const start = index;
  index = scanFence(lines, start);
  if (PROMPT_FENCE.test(line) && index > start + 1) {
    // Only prompt fences are split - body is prose, fence markers are preserved
    segments.push({
      kind: "prompt",
      opening: lines[start],
      body: lines.slice(start + 1, index - 1).join("\n"),
      closing: lines[index - 1],
    });
  } else {
    // ALL OTHER FENCES (including code examples) are marked as preserve
    segments.push({ kind: "preserve", text: lines.slice(start, index).join("\n") });
  }
  continue;
}
```

So the problem is:
1. Prompt fences (```prompt) have their body translated as prose, fence markers preserved
2. ALL OTHER fences (including html, javascript, css, etc.) are marked as `preserve` - emitted verbatim

But for cssdoc examples (html fences), the opening line often has a caption like:
```
```html A button with a primary color
...code...
```
```

The whole fence block is being marked as `preserve`, so the caption isn't being extracted and translated.

In contrast, demos appear to be working because they:
1. Store their prose in `demos/*/i18n.json` which gets translated to `l10n/<locale>/docs.demos.po`
2. The HTML files themselves are cloned/localized to `docs/<locale>/demos/*.html`
3. Then `demoMarkdownIt` loads the locale-specific demo when rendering

Now let me check where the cssdoc examples are currently being generated and whether there's special handling for their captions.
